st be  possible to find a way to to bridge that  remaining  two percent but one of the sticking  points seems to be  boris johnson wanting the powers to do  something he claims he doesn't want to  do anyway which makes the whole thing  more surreal you know he's arguing for  the powers to water down  employment rights and environmental  standards now  labor doesn't want to see that happen  the country doesn't want to see that  that happen so there is a deal to be  done here as one of your  your earlier interviewees was saying  economies are interdependent  uh these days we have to take shared  decisions if we're going to trade  with each other and taking a shared  decision is not a breach of  sovereignty it's exercising sovereignty  we have to get this deal  over the line we have to complete the  remaining two percent if that's all that  remains to be done  and we need to get this deal done so the  british economy and the british people  after four years of uncertainty can move  forwards  sure but there is clearly though a  difference between the interdependence  as described by the by the spanish  foreign minister  and the idea that's being pushed by this  government which you know if we accept  the deal that is on the table  we would be supplicants to the european  union  i don't think that's true this is about  taking shared decisions now  boris johnson is arguing at this very  late stage  that he wants the powers to water down  employment rights  and reduce environmental standards and  environmental protections  these are things he claims he doesn't  want to do anyway so it shouldn't really  be  a sticking point there's a deal to be  done if the if what the european union  wants to achieve  and what the british government wants to  achieve are the same uh which is what  they are both saying then this is  this is a surreal position we've got  ourselves in where he is preparing boris  johnson is preparing  if he if he breaks his promise to get  brexit done uh and 